---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="5.1 Introduction to Operations Management">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit5/">Unit 5</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>5.1 Introduction to Operations Management</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">5.1</span>
    <h1>Introduction to Operations Management</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Turning inputs into outputs, and why every operations decision lands on marketing, HR and finance.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'worked')">Worked Examples</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Explain the role of operations management in producing goods and providing services</li>
        <li>Describe the transformation process: inputs, process, outputs</li>
        <li>Identify the factors of production a business uses, and explain what changing the mix would do</li>
        <li>Explain how operations decisions affect, and are affected by, marketing, human resources and finance</li>
        <li>Link operations decisions to economies of scale and the sectors of industry</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">So what is Operations Management?</h2>
      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">Key Term</div>
        <p><strong>Operations management</strong> is the business function responsible for transforming inputs into outputs (goods or services) to meet business objectives.</p>
      </div>
      <p>It does not work in isolation. Every operations decision affects, and is affected by, marketing, HR, and finance. That is the single most useful idea in this topic: if a business changes <em>how</em> it makes something, it has also changed who it needs to employ, what it can charge, and what it has to spend.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">The Transformation Process</h2>
      <p>Operations involves three stages:</p>

      <div class="transform-flow">
        <div class="transform-step">
          <div class="step-label">Inputs</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">The factors of production:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Land</strong>: site, raw materials, natural resources</li>
            <li><strong>Labour</strong>: workers and their skills</li>
            <li><strong>Capital</strong>: machinery, buildings, technology</li>
            <li><strong>Enterprise</strong>: the decision to combine the others and take the risk</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="transform-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="transform-step">
          <div class="step-label">Process</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;">What is actually done to the inputs: mixing, baking, assembling, packaging, teaching, treating, advising, coding.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="transform-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="transform-step">
          <div class="step-label">Output</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;">The finished good or service, plus the data that comes back with it: sales, defect rates, customer feedback, delivery times.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Why this is worth memorising</div>
        <p>The three stages give you three things to analyse. How might the <strong>inputs</strong> change? How might the <strong>process</strong> change? What data in the <strong>output</strong> would you look for to get useful feedback? A question that looks like it has nothing to say usually has three angles once you use this.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Notice that this may or may not shift through the levels of industry:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Sector</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Extracts or grows raw materials</td><td>Wheat farming, mining, fishing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Secondary</strong></td><td>Manufactures raw materials into goods</td><td>Baking wafers, assembling cars</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Tertiary</strong></td><td>Provides services</td><td>Retail, transport, banking</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Quaternary</strong></td><td>Knowledge, information, research</td><td>R&amp;D, consultancy, data analysis</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Operations and the Other Business Functions</h2>
      <p>An operations decision is never only an operations decision. Use this table as a checklist when you are asked to evaluate one.</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Function</th><th>What operations does to it</th><th>What it does back to operations</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Marketing</strong></td>
            <td>Decides what can actually be promised: quality, lead time, customisation, country of origin, price point</td>
            <td>Sales forecasts and positioning set the volume and variety operations must deliver</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Human resources</strong></td>
            <td>Determines how many workers are needed, with what skills, and how they are trained and organised</td>
            <td>Labour availability, skill levels and motivation limit what operations can realistically run</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Finance</strong></td>
            <td>Drives capital spending, unit costs, working capital tied up in stock, and cash flow timing</td>
            <td>Available finance and required returns decide which operations options are affordable</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Something to notice about economies of scale</h2>
      <p>When a business expands capacity, ask what happens to the cost of producing each unit. Doubling capacity spreads fixed costs (the factory, the machinery, management) over far more units, so average cost per unit can fall. That is <strong>economies of scale</strong>, from Unit 1.6.</p>
      <p>But it could go either way. If the extra capacity is not filled, those same fixed costs are spread over too few units and cost per unit rises. Very large operations can also hit <strong>diseconomies of scale</strong>: communication gets slower, coordination gets harder, and motivation can fall.</p>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Exam Tip</div>
        <p>"It will benefit from economies of scale" is a weak point on its own. Make it conditional: <em>if</em> demand supports the extra volume, unit costs fall because fixed costs are spread more thinly, which lets the business either widen margins or price more competitively.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Key Terms</h2>
      <div class="key-terms">
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Operations management</div><div class="definition">The business function that transforms inputs into outputs to meet business objectives.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Transformation process</div><div class="definition">Converting inputs (factors of production) into finished goods or services.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Factors of production</div><div class="definition">Land, labour, capital and enterprise: the inputs a business combines to produce.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Capital intensive</div><div class="definition">Production that relies mainly on machinery and technology rather than labour.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Labour intensive</div><div class="definition">Production that relies mainly on workers rather than machinery.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Economies of scale</div><div class="definition">The fall in average cost per unit as output rises, mainly by spreading fixed costs over more units.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap: what you should know</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Operations transforms inputs into outputs to meet business objectives</li>
        <li>Inputs are the factors of production: land, labour, capital, enterprise</li>
        <li>The process is what is done to those inputs; the output includes the feedback data</li>
        <li>Changing the mix of inputs changes speed, cost, flexibility and variety</li>
        <li>Every operations decision has marketing, HR and finance consequences: say what they are, specifically</li>
        <li>Expanding capacity can lower unit costs through economies of scale, but only if the capacity is used</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- WORKED EXAMPLES -->
  <div id="worked" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Worked Example: one operations decision, three knock-on effects</div>

      <p style="font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--text-muted); margin-bottom:1rem;">
        Bauducco, a Brazilian baked goods company, opened its biggest US manufacturing facility in Tampa Bay, Florida: a $200 million investment creating around 600 jobs and doubling its US production capacity.
        <a href="https://www.foodbusinessnews.net/articles/30592-bauducco-launches-biggest-us-manufacturing-facility" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the article</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Operations to Marketing</div>
        <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
          <p><strong>From the article:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>"Made in USA" positioning becomes available</li>
            <li>Shorter lead times and greater supply chain reliability improve retail responsiveness</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Speculative (clearly signalled as such):</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Local production could support new flavour and format launches faster</li>
            <li>Risk of losing the "imported / authentic Brazilian" appeal</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Operations to HR</div>
        <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
          <p><strong>From the article:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>600 new jobs created in Tampa Bay</li>
            <li>Staffing an entirely new facility</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Speculative:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Need for training in new production technology</li>
            <li>Possible recruitment challenges in a competitive local labour market</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Operations to Finance</div>
        <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
          <p><strong>From the article:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>$200 million capital investment in the facility</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Speculative:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Investment appraisal required to justify the spend (payback, ARR, NPV)</li>
            <li>The financing method (loan, retained profit, share issue) affects gearing and risk</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Exam Tip</div>
        <p>Separate what the stimulus actually tells you from what you are inferring. Points taken straight from the case earn application marks; sensible inferences earn analysis marks, but only if you signal them ("this may mean that...") rather than inventing facts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /worked -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Practice Exercises</div>
      <p class="reveal-hint">Hover over a blurred box to reveal the answer, or tap it on a phone or tablet.</p>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          1. Read and discuss
          <p>Read <a href="https://www.foodbusinessnews.net/articles/30592-bauducco-launches-biggest-us-manufacturing-facility" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bauducco launches biggest US manufacturing facility</a> (Food Business News). In groups:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>What is the company doing?</li>
            <li>How is this about operations?</li>
            <li>How does this relate to marketing, HR and finance?</li>
          </ol>
          <p>Be as specific as possible. Use specific parts of each topic, not just "it affects marketing".</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          2. Apply it: Bauducco's transformation process
          <p>Identify the factors of production, the process, and the output for Bauducco. The <a href="https://www.bauducco.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bauducco website</a> may help. Draw it in the inputs / process / output format from the notes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Possible answer</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            <strong>Factors of production:</strong> land (the Florida site), capital (machinery and the facility itself), labour (the 600 jobs), enterprise (Bauducco's decision to invest).<br />
            <strong>Process:</strong> mixing, baking, layering wafers, packaging.<br />
            <strong>Output:</strong> wafers, cookies, panettones.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          3. Change the mix
          <p>What would it mean to change these factors? How could the company "mix" them differently? Draw two more versions of the process, making sure each step stays consistent with the others. For the output, draw on some of the marketing mix.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Possible answer</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            More machinery and less labour would speed up production and cut unit costs, but reduce flexibility: harder to switch to a different product or a special edition.<br />
            More skilled labour could support product variety and customisation (regional flavours, seasonal panettone), but raises costs per unit and makes recruitment and training a bigger constraint.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          4. Economies of scale
          <p>This facility doubles production capacity. What might that mean for the cost of producing each wafer? Link to economies of scale, Unit 1.6.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Possible answer</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            It could go either way. If the extra capacity is filled, fixed costs (the $200m facility, machinery, management) are spread over roughly twice as many units, so average cost per wafer falls: technical and purchasing economies of scale. If demand does not grow to match, the same fixed costs sit on too little output and cost per wafer rises. Scale on its own does not guarantee lower unit costs; utilisation does.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          5. Operations decisions in action
          <p>Work individually through the mini case studies, returning to how each decision affects marketing, HR and finance: <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1xrbd9gNDrsGPky0GiXwYTuxKXpe5uXGd0Yqt2YQKXb0/edit?tab=t.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IB2 BM Operations Decisions in Action: Mini Case Studies 26/27</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Consolidation game: Guess the Business</div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem; margin-bottom:1rem;">
        For each business, five clues are read one at a time, from vague to obvious. Guess as soon as you think you know: you earn more points for guessing early.
        <br /><strong>Scoring:</strong> 5 points after clue 1, 4 after clue 2, 3 after clue 3, 2 after clue 4, 1 after clue 5.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal-hint">Reveal each answer only once your group has committed to a guess.</p>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Business 1</div>
        <ol>
          <li>We take raw materials and skilled labour to create something people use every day.</li>
          <li>Our main input is a mix of chemicals, natural extracts, and packaging materials.</li>
          <li>Our process involves precise mixing, heating, and quality testing in a lab-like environment.</li>
          <li>Our output includes creams, lotions, and serums sold in bottles and jars.</li>
          <li>You would find our products on a bathroom shelf, applied to the face or body every morning.</li>
        </ol>
        <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A skincare / cosmetics manufacturer</span></button>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Business 2</div>
        <ol>
          <li>Our inputs include people, buildings, and a lot of information.</li>
          <li>We do not make a physical product. Our "output" is a change in someone's knowledge.</li>
          <li>Our process involves planning, delivering, and assessing structured sessions.</li>
          <li>Our main resources are qualified staff and a fixed daily timetable.</li>
          <li>Our "customers" are aged between 11 and 18, and attend us five days a week, term time only.</li>
        </ol>
        <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A school</span></button>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Business 3</div>
        <ol>
          <li>Our inputs are grown, not manufactured.</li>
          <li>We rely heavily on land, weather, and seasonal labour.</li>
          <li>Our process involves harvesting, sorting, and packaging.</li>
          <li>Our output is sold in supermarkets, often within days of leaving our site.</li>
          <li>Our product grows on trees, bushes or in the ground, and ends up in a fruit bowl.</li>
        </ol>
        <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A farm</span></button>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Business 4</div>
        <ol>
          <li>Our inputs include metal, plastic, and electronic components.</li>
          <li>We use a combination of skilled labour and heavy machinery.</li>
          <li>Our process involves assembling thousands of small parts into one large product.</li>
          <li>Our output needs to pass strict safety and performance testing before leaving the factory.</li>
          <li>Our output has four wheels, an engine, and needs a licence to drive.</li>
        </ol>
        <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A car manufacturer</span></button>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Business 5</div>
        <ol>
          <li>Our main input is dough, sugar, and flavourings.</li>
          <li>We operate large ovens and precise mixing equipment.</li>
          <li>Our process must happen quickly, since our product does not last long.</li>
          <li>Our output is sold in individual portions, often branded and packaged colourfully.</li>
          <li>Our snacks come in layers, are crisp, and are sold in flavours like chocolate, vanilla, strawberry and coconut.</li>
        </ol>
        <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Bauducco (yes, the same company as the opening article)</span></button>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

